00:42:45.840 --> 00:42:49.480
So I'm just going to call
this the auto routing agent.

00:42:54.750 --> 00:42:58.560
Just so we can take a
look at what it's doing.

00:42:58.560 --> 00:43:02.990
And the interesting thing that
you have to understand here is

00:43:02.990 --> 00:43:05.690
in the original case, we
started with a single model,

00:43:05.690 --> 00:43:09.200
we've compared it, figured out
a good model based on quality

00:43:09.200 --> 00:43:11.780
and costs, and we replaced it.

00:43:11.780 --> 00:43:13.550
But we were looking at just one

00:43:13.550 --> 00:43:16.400
or two use cases,
simple questions.

00:43:16.400 --> 00:43:18.860
In the real world, I might
have tons of different kinds

00:43:18.860 --> 00:43:21.050
of questions of different
layers of complexity.

00:43:21.050 --> 00:43:22.520
How do I know the right
model for the job?

00:43:22.520 --> 00:43:25.190
I can sit there and test each
one using the leaderboard,

00:43:25.190 --> 00:43:28.830
or I can deploy model
router and have it show me

00:43:28.830 --> 00:43:30.420
which models are being used.

00:43:30.420 --> 00:43:32.340
That's what we're going
to try to do here.

00:43:32.340 --> 00:43:34.440
So the first thing I'm going
to do is I'm actually going

00:43:34.440 --> 00:43:36.780
to go back to our retail
base agent and I'm going

00:43:36.780 --> 00:43:39.930
to take the same instructions.

00:43:39.930 --> 00:43:42.420
I'll put it into this
new one, but we're going

00:43:42.420 --> 00:43:44.160
to do a really
simple thing here.

00:43:44.160 --> 00:43:46.260
We're going to save that.

00:43:46.260 --> 00:43:48.960
And now we're going to keep
asking it a lot of prompts

00:43:48.960 --> 00:43:50.700
with higher levels
of complexity.

00:43:50.700 --> 00:43:53.160
So the very first one, we can
ask a very simple question like,

00:43:53.160 --> 00:43:56.580
hey, what is the weather?

00:43:56.580 --> 00:44:00.490
What is the capital of France?

00:44:01.620 --> 00:44:04.410
Okay, very simple question.

00:44:04.410 --> 00:44:07.140
So Model Router will try to
figure out the right model

00:44:07.140 --> 00:44:10.380
for the job and actually
send me the response back.

00:44:10.380 --> 00:44:13.710
And it came up with, hey,
it's a very trivial question.

00:44:13.710 --> 00:44:16.500
GPDM is good enough, right,
so it sends it there.

00:44:16.500 --> 00:44:22.140
Then I can say, I want to
build a patio during summer

00:44:22.140 --> 00:44:33.870
for a family of five
with plants that grow.

00:44:33.870 --> 00:44:35.940
Anything like this, right.

00:44:35.940 --> 00:44:39.450
Draw me up a plan of action.

00:44:39.450 --> 00:44:43.230
So now I've given it a much
more complicated task, right.

00:44:43.230 --> 00:44:44.820
I'm not just saying,
hey, what does our have?

00:44:44.820 --> 00:44:48.720
I'm saying, draw me up a
plan of how I do this.

00:44:48.720 --> 00:44:50.100
This is the kind of thing

00:44:50.100 --> 00:44:54.000
that would require a higher
performance model to reason

00:44:54.000 --> 00:44:55.770
and give me back a response.

00:44:55.770 --> 00:44:57.510
Let's see what
Model Router does.

00:44:57.510 --> 00:44:58.950
Remember, though,
that throughout this,

00:44:58.950 --> 00:44:59.970
I never changed the route.

00:44:59.970 --> 00:45:01.020
I now changed my model.

00:45:01.020 --> 00:45:04.530
I had a single model, but
it routed to GPT-5.1 mini,

00:45:04.530 --> 00:45:06.660
I mean GPT-5 mini
the first time.

00:45:06.660 --> 00:45:09.180
And let's see what it
routes to this time

00:45:09.180 --> 00:45:11.440
when it gives us
back a response.

00:45:12.840 --> 00:45:14.040
Awesome. So you can see

00:45:14.040 --> 00:45:16.770
that this time it took a lot
more time, but it came back

00:45:16.770 --> 00:45:19.170
with a fairly
comprehensive plan.

00:45:19.170 --> 00:45:21.930
Let's see what model it used.

00:45:21.930 --> 00:45:23.550
It's still going.

00:45:23.550 --> 00:45:26.310
Now, this is not just
like a quick answer,

00:45:26.310 --> 00:45:29.400
it's a fairly comprehensive
minute long answer that took

00:45:29.400 --> 00:45:31.050
up a lot of tokens and note

00:45:31.050 --> 00:45:33.720
that it picked a higher
performance model,

00:45:33.720 --> 00:45:37.170
GPT-5 versus mini, and that's
the power Model Router.

00:45:37.170 --> 00:45:39.600
So keep that in mind that
when you're starting,

00:45:39.600 --> 00:45:42.180
if you're in your test kitchen,
if you've already started

00:45:42.180 --> 00:45:46.200
with an ingredient and you've
started with a chef and you want

00:45:46.200 --> 00:45:47.790
to say, hey, this is a
task someone else can do,

00:45:47.790 --> 00:45:49.110
you can hand it off to
them, that's one thing.

00:45:49.110 --> 00:45:52.770
But if you didn't know ahead
of time what the complexity

00:45:52.770 --> 00:45:54.720
of the task is, you
can effectively go

00:45:54.720 --> 00:45:57.780
to the kitchen organizer,
the Model Router, and say,

00:45:57.780 --> 00:45:58.560
hey, I have the stuff.

00:45:58.560 --> 00:46:00.450
Do you know who in this
kitchen will be good for it?

00:46:00.450 --> 00:46:03.510
And it'll figure it out and send
it to the right model for you.

00:46:03.510 --> 00:46:05.940
If you kind of run this over a
period of time, you'll be able

00:46:05.940 --> 00:46:09.390
to see which models are
most used for your tasks

00:46:09.390 --> 00:46:11.310
and make that the default.

00:46:11.310 --> 00:46:13.230
All right, so with
