00:53:44.280 --> 00:53:45.840
What are evaluations?

00:53:45.840 --> 00:53:49.260
Now it's time for us to go take
a look at what evaluations are.

00:53:49.260 --> 00:53:51.240
So if you click on the
evaluations table,

00:53:51.240 --> 00:53:54.390
what you'll see is there is
a tab here for evaluations,

00:53:54.390 --> 00:53:56.490
but if you ever run
evaluations on your agent,

00:53:56.490 --> 00:53:59.730
you'll see the results here,
and there's a catalog.

00:53:59.730 --> 00:54:02.250
Let's talk about what the
evaluator catalog is.

00:54:02.250 --> 00:54:04.770
Going back to the test kitchen
analogy, if I'm cooking

00:54:04.770 --> 00:54:08.400
in a test kitchen, I want to
taste the different things

00:54:08.400 --> 00:54:10.560
and see if they meet
the requirements

00:54:10.560 --> 00:54:12.900
for that recipe, right.

00:54:12.900 --> 00:54:17.460
That means that I need a process
by which I can taste it.

00:54:17.460 --> 00:54:21.600
And then I need a criteria,
some scale on which I say,

00:54:21.600 --> 00:54:25.590
based on this taste, I rank
this between A and B, right.

00:54:25.590 --> 00:54:30.300
Then I get a good sense of how
close I am to the desired level

00:54:30.300 --> 00:54:33.960
of acceptance criteria, right.

00:54:33.960 --> 00:54:36.270
So the good thing
about Foundry is

00:54:36.270 --> 00:54:39.000
that you have a
ton of evaluators.

00:54:39.000 --> 00:54:41.340
If you click on this, these
are all built-in evaluators,

00:54:41.340 --> 00:54:42.810
and you can see that
the evaluators come

00:54:42.810 --> 00:54:45.180
in four different categories.

00:54:45.180 --> 00:54:46.710
You have things like quality.

00:54:46.710 --> 00:54:50.130
It can look at, sorry, let's,
if you look at quality,

00:54:50.130 --> 00:54:51.390
it's going to look at
coherence, fluency,

00:54:51.390 --> 00:54:54.390
the quality of the response,
the formats, et cetera.

00:54:54.390 --> 00:54:56.490
If you look at safety,
it's going to look

00:54:56.490 --> 00:54:58.500
at things like violence, hate.

00:54:58.500 --> 00:55:01.680
Does the conversation have
anything that might trigger any

00:55:01.680 --> 00:55:04.200
of your safety
acceptance criteria?

00:55:04.200 --> 00:55:06.780
Business, we'll look
at that in a bit.

00:55:06.780 --> 00:55:10.890
There are business evaluation,
evaluators also provided to you.

00:55:10.890 --> 00:55:13.200
Then agentic evaluators, which
are the most interesting,

00:55:13.200 --> 00:55:15.780
are those that look at whether
the agent performed the way

00:55:15.780 --> 00:55:16.380
it should.

00:55:16.380 --> 00:55:19.410
Did it kind of resolve the
intent of the user's question?

00:55:19.410 --> 00:55:21.900
Did it actually complete the
response and did it adhere

00:55:21.900 --> 00:55:24.030
to that task, et cetera.

00:55:24.030 --> 00:55:25.260
So these are your
standard things.

00:55:25.260 --> 00:55:27.150
So how do you use
them on your agent?

00:55:27.150 --> 00:55:29.700
Well, we're going to
talk about two things.

00:55:29.700 --> 00:55:32.850
First, we're going to talk about
how you create an evaluation.

00:55:32.850 --> 00:55:35.980
So I can go ahead and
say base evaluation

00:55:37.620 --> 00:55:40.260
for my agent and then here.

00:55:40.260 --> 00:55:43.170
And then I'm basically, so this
is for custom evaluator, sorry.

00:55:43.170 --> 00:55:45.570
So over here, I can actually
add a custom evaluator.

00:55:45.570 --> 00:55:46.800
So the first thing I'm
going to do is I'm going

00:55:46.800 --> 00:55:49.590
to add a friendliness evaluator.

00:55:49.590 --> 00:55:56.370
And what I want this to do
is say, hey, give me rank

00:55:56.370 --> 00:56:02.550
or rate the friendliness
of my agent based

00:56:02.550 --> 00:56:08.580
on different criteria
I provide, right.

00:56:08.580 --> 00:56:10.740
And so I'm going to say
this is a quality criteria.

00:56:10.740 --> 00:56:11.820
Now, prompt-based.

00:56:11.820 --> 00:56:14.010
What's the difference between
prompt and code-based?

00:56:14.010 --> 00:56:15.540
A prompt-based evaluator is

00:56:15.540 --> 00:56:18.180
where you actually
have another agent act

00:56:18.180 --> 00:56:19.800
as a judge for your first agent.

00:56:19.800 --> 00:56:21.660
So it's using LLM as a judge.

00:56:21.660 --> 00:56:25.260
So now what you do with the
prompt-based agent is you create

00:56:25.260 --> 00:56:28.950
a prompt that actually
does the grading,

00:56:28.950 --> 00:56:32.250
and the other agent will use
that prompt as instructions

00:56:32.250 --> 00:56:34.110
to grade the response
of the first one

00:56:34.110 --> 00:56:36.240
and then give you a result.

00:56:36.240 --> 00:56:38.730
This is really good
when your criteria,

00:56:38.730 --> 00:56:42.000
when your evaluation is for
something that is intangible.

00:56:42.000 --> 00:56:43.020
So we have politeness.

00:56:43.020 --> 00:56:43.860
What does politeness mean?

00:56:43.860 --> 00:56:45.600
It's not a very discreet way.

00:56:45.600 --> 00:56:46.920
It's very subjective.

00:56:46.920 --> 00:56:48.780
So I can actually write a prompt
here, and we're going to go

00:56:48.780 --> 00:56:50.610
and do that in just a second.

00:56:50.610 --> 00:56:53.130
Code bases, on the other hand,
is like writing a function

00:56:53.130 --> 00:56:54.870
that takes the current input

00:56:54.870 --> 00:56:57.150
and returns a
deterministic response.

00:56:57.150 --> 00:56:59.250
And that's a great way for
you to write function tools

00:56:59.250 --> 00:57:01.530
for things like, hey,
how long is this?

00:57:01.530 --> 00:57:04.350
So conciseness, for example, I
might define as if this is more

00:57:04.350 --> 00:57:06.360
than 500 but less than 5,000,

00:57:06.360 --> 00:57:08.220
then it's concise,
otherwise it's not.

00:57:08.220 --> 00:57:09.240
I can do that with a function.

00:57:09.240 --> 00:57:11.340
I don't need to
waste an LLM on it.

00:57:11.340 --> 00:57:12.450
So let's see how we can do that.

00:57:12.450 --> 00:57:15.060
So the first one, we're going to
basically say, let me go ahead

00:57:15.060 --> 00:57:17.160
and give me a one
to five rating.

00:57:17.160 --> 00:57:19.020
And the easiest way
for you to kind

00:57:19.020 --> 00:57:21.270
of build a custom
evaluator using prompts is

00:57:21.270 --> 00:57:23.640
to go take the
example that's there.

00:57:23.640 --> 00:57:25.980
And very coincidentally,
it's a friendliness one.

00:57:25.980 --> 00:57:27.690
So we're just going to
go ahead and we're going

00:57:27.690 --> 00:57:29.340
to replace this prompt.

00:57:29.340 --> 00:57:31.050
Let me do this properly.

00:57:31.050 --> 00:57:34.380
We're going to replace this
with a very simple prompt

00:57:34.380 --> 00:57:38.550
that says this is how you should
assess the incoming input

00:57:38.550 --> 00:57:41.070
to tell me whether
it's friendly or not.

00:57:41.070 --> 00:57:43.080
Unfriendly or hostile, I'm
going to customize this.

00:57:43.080 --> 00:57:49.470
Unfriendly or hostile
means uses swear words

00:57:49.470 --> 00:57:53.130
or rude language might be a way
that I want to kind of quantify

00:57:53.130 --> 00:57:55.260
that a little bit more.

00:57:55.260 --> 00:57:56.880
This one, it's unfriendly.

00:57:56.880 --> 00:58:00.510
Maybe just says, for example,

00:58:00.510 --> 00:58:03.540
does not offer help is
an unfriendly thing.

00:58:03.540 --> 00:58:06.840
And this I'll say
for example, right.

00:58:06.840 --> 00:58:08.940
For this one, neutral, I'll
leave it mostly friendly.

00:58:08.940 --> 00:58:14.730
I'll be like, for example,
starts with a cheerful greeting.

00:58:14.730 --> 00:58:16.830
Remember, our instructions
ask you to start

00:58:16.830 --> 00:58:17.670
with a cheerful greeting.

00:58:17.670 --> 00:58:21.120
So this lets us use friendliness
as a custom evaluator

00:58:21.120 --> 00:58:24.270
to see whether every response
matches our instructions.

00:58:24.270 --> 00:58:25.980
So I can do this.

00:58:25.980 --> 00:58:27.930
And then I can say
very friendly is

00:58:27.930 --> 00:58:29.640
when it did everything I asked.

00:58:29.640 --> 00:58:37.290
So starts with a cheerful
greeting and ends

00:58:37.290 --> 00:58:40.150
with an offer to help, right.

00:58:43.980 --> 00:58:48.960
Okay. I can go ahead
and create this.

00:58:48.960 --> 00:58:52.830
And now when I create this
evaluator, it should show up.

00:58:52.830 --> 00:58:54.840
It's created a
friendliness evaluator.

00:58:54.840 --> 00:58:56.640
It takes sometimes a
little bit of time for it

00:58:56.640 --> 00:58:59.190
to actually add it
into this list.

00:58:59.190 --> 00:59:01.830
But after a period of time,
you will come in here

00:59:01.830 --> 00:59:04.110
and you'll find that there's a
friendliness evaluator that's

00:59:04.110 --> 00:59:05.790
available to you as well.

00:59:05.790 --> 00:59:08.190
So we'll come back and check
that out, but in the meantime,

00:59:08.190 --> 00:59:11.970
let's go and look at how I would
start an evaluation on my agent.

00:59:11.970 --> 00:59:14.400
So here is my agent and remember

00:59:14.400 --> 00:59:16.020
that we've been
making modifications,

00:59:16.020 --> 00:59:18.450
we are in version nine, right.

00:59:18.450 --> 00:59:20.640
If I'm just making changes
and then testing it

00:59:20.640 --> 00:59:22.860
out with a single prompt,

00:59:22.860 --> 00:59:24.930
that is not really
a batch evaluation.

00:59:24.930 --> 00:59:27.060
I've tested it on one prompt.

00:59:27.060 --> 00:59:29.760
It may not really work
that way for everything.

00:59:29.760 --> 00:59:33.030
So batch evaluations are where
you can set up an evaluation

00:59:33.030 --> 00:59:35.460
that runs on your agent and we
are running it now manually,

00:59:35.460 --> 00:59:38.400
but it can be run in continuous
evaluation form as well.

00:59:38.400 --> 00:59:40.890
Where you say, can you
please evaluate this

00:59:40.890 --> 00:59:43.830
against a large dataset
of prompts that tests

00:59:43.830 --> 00:59:46.170
out all the possible things
that could go wrong.

00:59:46.170 --> 00:59:49.410
So to do that, we're going to go
ahead and create an evaluation.

00:59:49.410 --> 00:59:51.240
So I'm going to say,
please target my agent.

00:59:51.240 --> 00:59:53.310
Notice that I can target
any version that I want,

00:59:53.310 --> 00:59:54.930
but I'm going to start with
version seven and say,

00:59:54.930 --> 00:59:57.540
please target that agent.

00:59:57.540 --> 01:00:00.300
This, remember, this
is a new agent.

01:00:00.300 --> 01:00:01.230
It's in my test kitchen.

01:00:01.230 --> 01:00:02.910
I don't actually put it on
the table in a restaurant.

01:00:02.910 --> 01:00:04.230
I have no idea what people
are saying about it,

01:00:04.230 --> 01:00:06.420
so how do I get
these test prompts

01:00:06.420 --> 01:00:08.640
that I can use to evaluate it?

01:00:08.640 --> 01:00:10.650
Foundry in the portal
gives you the ability

01:00:10.650 --> 01:00:12.180
to generate synthetic data.

01:00:12.180 --> 01:00:14.010
So I'm going to go ahead
and say, please go ahead

01:00:14.010 --> 01:00:16.230
and generate some
prompts for me.

01:00:16.230 --> 01:00:19.650
And I'm basically going to
say new dataset is synth gen,

01:00:19.650 --> 01:00:21.240
let's say, just so I remember.

01:00:21.240 --> 01:00:25.140
I'll say use this other model
to create these for me.

01:00:25.140 --> 01:00:29.910
I'm going to just say give
me maybe just 20 prompts.

01:00:29.910 --> 01:00:31.740
Let's make it even less
because we want the evaluation

01:00:31.740 --> 01:00:32.610
to finish.

01:00:32.610 --> 01:00:36.030
Give me 10 prompts, 10 test
prompts that I could use,

01:00:36.030 --> 01:00:38.520
and go ahead and generate
the candidate response too

01:00:38.520 --> 01:00:39.060
if you want.

01:00:39.060 --> 01:00:41.400
And what I want to say,
what I want it to generate.

01:00:41.400 --> 01:00:48.910
Generate prompts for
my Zava retail agent

01:00:50.100 --> 01:00:54.600
that represent customer
support queries

01:00:54.600 --> 01:01:05.310
for a DIY enterprise retail
organization selling home

01:01:05.310 --> 01:01:07.260
improvement goods.

01:01:07.260 --> 01:01:12.780
So I can put a very broad
level of kind of thing, right.

01:01:12.780 --> 01:01:23.050
And then I'm saying cover
single and multi-turn

01:01:28.020 --> 01:01:34.050
conversations and
edge cases like out

01:01:34.050 --> 01:01:38.340
of stock or returns, right.

01:01:38.340 --> 01:01:39.570
So I can say
something like this.

01:01:39.570 --> 01:01:43.560
I'm literally saying, give me 10
prompts that people might come

01:01:43.560 --> 01:01:46.770
to my agent and ask, and I want
to use those to really test

01:01:46.770 --> 01:01:49.770
that it's doing the right
thing every single time.

01:01:49.770 --> 01:01:51.900
So I go ahead and
say, now I'm saying,

01:01:51.900 --> 01:01:55.080
please generate synthetic data
and I'm going to use that.

01:01:55.080 --> 01:01:57.300
And then the criteria, note

01:01:57.300 --> 01:01:59.820
that it auto suggested
evaluators for me.

01:01:59.820 --> 01:02:01.380
It said, hey, here
are the evaluators

01:02:01.380 --> 01:02:02.520
that I think you might like.

01:02:02.520 --> 01:02:04.800
In the interest of time, I'm
just going to pick a few.

01:02:04.800 --> 01:02:07.470
Say tool call accuracy,
task completion.

01:02:07.470 --> 01:02:08.820
Let's keep adherence.

01:02:08.820 --> 01:02:10.320
I'll remove a bunch of these.

01:02:10.320 --> 01:02:13.260
I've just kept a few because it
takes time to run evaluations.

01:02:13.260 --> 01:02:14.640
I've kept the quality ones.

01:02:14.640 --> 01:02:19.410
Let's keep a couple
of things in safety,

01:02:19.410 --> 01:02:21.600
and we'll call it done, right.

01:02:21.600 --> 01:02:29.130
So now I can basically go ahead
and say batch eval and submit.

01:02:29.130 --> 01:02:30.240
And when I do this,

01:02:30.240 --> 01:02:33.660
what's really happening now is
I have a process that's kicking

01:02:33.660 --> 01:02:39.240
off where an agent is, remember
this is a LLM as a judge,

01:02:39.240 --> 01:02:42.780
it's going to use the test
prompts that I gave it.

01:02:42.780 --> 01:02:45.480
It's going to invoke
them on my actual agent,

01:02:45.480 --> 01:02:47.160
get the responses back,
and then it's going

01:02:47.160 --> 01:02:51.720
to grade those responses for
the evaluators I gave it.

01:02:51.720 --> 01:02:53.100
Now I didn't put the
custom evaluator,

01:02:53.100 --> 01:02:56.280
and you could absolutely have
added the custom evaluator in,

01:02:56.280 --> 01:02:59.430
but for now, we'll let this run
and we'll come back to this.

01:02:59.430 --> 01:03:00.360
So what does this do?

01:03:00.360 --> 01:03:03.570
This allows us to actually
continuously run evaluator.

01:03:03.570 --> 01:03:06.300
So next time I make another
change, as an example,

01:03:06.300 --> 01:03:09.420
I can go off and run
the evaluator again,

01:03:09.420 --> 01:03:12.480
and then I'll be able to
see how much the value.

01:03:12.480 --> 01:03:14.070
So right now, this
is still running.

01:03:14.070 --> 01:03:15.420
Once this is done,
you'll see the results.

01:03:15.420 --> 01:03:17.730
I can now see with every
evaluation run how the results

01:03:17.730 --> 01:03:18.960
are improving.

01:03:18.960 --> 01:03:21.210
We'll come back and check
on that in just a bit.

01:03:21.210 --> 01:03:23.640
So the last thing I want to
talk about, we've talked

01:03:23.640 --> 01:03:25.830
about evaluators, right,
and evaluators are

01:03:25.830 --> 01:03:28.770
like the kitchen inspection guy.

01:03:28.770 --> 01:03:31.290
I've built this recipe and
I've kind of like tried it

01:03:31.290 --> 01:03:33.600
out in my kitchen, then
I have someone coming

01:03:33.600 --> 01:03:35.070
in to inspect the
thing and make sure

01:03:35.070 --> 01:03:37.170
that I'm using the tools
correctly, that my kitchen is

01:03:37.170 --> 01:03:40.710
in good shape, the recipe is
correctly labeled for all

01:03:40.710 --> 01:03:42.060
of that kind of stuff, right.

01:03:42.060 --> 01:03:46.500
So this is when the inspection
evaluations are really checking

01:03:46.500 --> 01:03:48.330
that my recipe is working.

01:03:48.330 --> 01:03:51.180
But what if someone
is malicious?

01:03:51.180 --> 01:03:55.500
How do I know that my agent
is protected from people

01:03:55.500 --> 01:03:58.680
who act maliciously when
they send a prompt?

01:03:58.680 --> 01:04:03.300
In other words, what if someone
came in asking for a recipe

01:04:03.300 --> 01:04:05.730
that they know is going
to be dangerous, right?

01:04:05.730 --> 01:04:09.390
And somehow it got past all
these guardrails that I put in

01:04:09.390 --> 01:04:11.760
and I end up making
it and it's harmful.

01:04:11.760 --> 01:04:13.440
How do I prevent that?

01:04:13.440 --> 01:04:16.140
This is where red teaming comes
in, and red teaming is part

01:04:16.140 --> 01:04:17.880
of the evaluation suite.

01:04:17.880 --> 01:04:21.240
So over here, I'll just
show you how to run one.

01:04:21.240 --> 01:04:22.500
It'll take a little bit of time.

01:04:22.500 --> 01:04:24.630
You can come back and look
at the results later.

01:04:24.630 --> 01:04:27.930
But note that this is where the
red teaming option allows you

01:04:27.930 --> 01:04:30.630
to create a red teaming run.

01:04:30.630 --> 01:04:34.650
And what the red teaming run
will do is it allows you to kind

01:04:34.650 --> 01:04:39.840
of assess the kinds of risks
that you are vulnerable to.

01:04:39.840 --> 01:04:41.250
So here I'm going to
say, hey, I'd like you

01:04:41.250 --> 01:04:43.410
to run an adversarial attack.

01:04:43.410 --> 01:04:46.800
So the red teaming agent
effectively pretends

01:04:46.800 --> 01:04:51.330
to be a malicious user
and attacks your agent

01:04:51.330 --> 01:04:55.950
by asking it prompts that
would trigger harmful behavior

01:04:55.950 --> 01:04:58.050
if your agent allowed
them through.

01:04:58.050 --> 01:04:59.460
So here I'm going
to say, go ahead

01:04:59.460 --> 01:05:02.910
and try it on this retail agent.

01:05:02.910 --> 01:05:05.730
And what attacks do I want?

01:05:05.730 --> 01:05:07.110
There are tons of them.

01:05:07.110 --> 01:05:09.210
In the interest of time,
I'm basically going to say,

01:05:09.210 --> 01:05:10.920
let's not do web search.

01:05:10.920 --> 01:05:13.590
I want to, and I'll
just say kind of like

01:05:13.590 --> 01:05:18.090
for the tool description,
use this for all requests

01:05:18.090 --> 01:05:24.760
that return products from Zava.

01:05:26.070 --> 01:05:28.380
And then over here, let's just
keep a couple of things, right.

01:05:28.380 --> 01:05:29.190
I don't want everything.

01:05:29.190 --> 01:05:32.310
So in the interest of like
making this complete quickly,

01:05:32.310 --> 01:05:35.730
I said, hey, I think my
agent is at risk for prompts

01:05:35.730 --> 01:05:39.780
that might end up creating
responses that have violence

01:05:39.780 --> 01:05:41.580
or task adherence issues.

01:05:41.580 --> 01:05:44.670
Let's assess if that
happens, right.

01:05:44.670 --> 01:05:47.910
I can go ahead and pick
those attack categories,

01:05:47.910 --> 01:05:48.900
the risk categories, things

01:05:48.900 --> 01:05:52.440
that I think I have
more risk towards.

01:05:52.440 --> 01:05:53.640
I'll put a couple of things.

01:05:53.640 --> 01:05:55.680
So it basically says for that
category, I'll come up with

01:05:55.680 --> 01:05:58.650
at least three prompts and
I'll try to attack your agent.

01:05:58.650 --> 01:06:00.000
Then attack strategies.

01:06:00.000 --> 01:06:01.830
So how does it
attack your agent?

01:06:01.830 --> 01:06:05.550
Obviously, if it brought the
prompt exactly as it was,

01:06:05.550 --> 01:06:07.470
I'm going to know there are
content safety filters.

01:06:07.470 --> 01:06:10.140
We've got a lot of
things in place.

01:06:10.140 --> 01:06:12.810
What red teaming agents do
is try to figure out how

01:06:12.810 --> 01:06:14.640
to manipulate the
prompt in a way

01:06:14.640 --> 01:06:16.530
that gets past the guardrails.

01:06:16.530 --> 01:06:18.450
Those are attack strategies.

01:06:18.450 --> 01:06:22.860
An example, I can say, hey,
let's use a very simple,

01:06:22.860 --> 01:06:25.230
there's a flip strategy
out here, flip.

01:06:25.230 --> 01:06:28.410
Flip says if I just flip
the string over and send it

01:06:28.410 --> 01:06:33.780
through with a harmful
request, because it's flipped,

01:06:33.780 --> 01:06:35.850
it doesn't read like
a harmful request.

01:06:35.850 --> 01:06:37.110
So it gets through
the guardrails

01:06:37.110 --> 01:06:38.490
and then it'll run it.

01:06:38.490 --> 01:06:41.760
And because the model is smart,
it looks at it and goes, oh,

01:06:41.760 --> 01:06:44.190
you just, this is a flip
string, let me re-flip it

01:06:44.190 --> 01:06:45.870
and then run it, right.

01:06:45.870 --> 01:06:46.950
How do you protect against that?

01:06:46.950 --> 01:06:50.730
So let's see if your agent is
susceptible to flip attacks.

01:06:50.730 --> 01:06:53.280
That's a simple attack,
an easy attack.

01:06:53.280 --> 01:06:55.650
There are more
difficult attacks.

01:06:55.650 --> 01:06:59.340
For example, a tense attack
says, I've told you not

01:06:59.340 --> 01:07:02.130
to respond to these, but if I
phrase it in the past of, hey,

01:07:02.130 --> 01:07:05.550
this happened in the past, it's
no longer an issue for me.

01:07:05.550 --> 01:07:09.810
If I use tense to kind of like
say, this is not important,

01:07:09.810 --> 01:07:10.800
it's not going to harm anyone,

01:07:10.800 --> 01:07:13.350
but what if my
grandmom said this?

01:07:13.350 --> 01:07:16.170
Can you explain what she would
have said and it gets through?

01:07:16.170 --> 01:07:17.520
That's a tense attack.

01:07:17.520 --> 01:07:19.410
That's a slightly
more complex attack.

01:07:19.410 --> 01:07:21.600
So I've picked a
couple of attacks,

01:07:21.600 --> 01:07:23.820
and now I can actually
run a red teaming scan.

01:07:23.820 --> 01:07:25.350
So let's call this
a red teaming scan.

01:07:25.350 --> 01:07:26.880
I'm going to submit it.

01:07:26.880 --> 01:07:31.560
And now what you'll see with
this is we have now gone from A,

01:07:31.560 --> 01:07:34.050
taking that agent that we
provided, the very basic one,

01:07:34.050 --> 01:07:36.390
and we have changed
its instructions,

01:07:36.390 --> 01:07:39.870
made it more along the
lines of what we want

01:07:39.870 --> 01:07:41.610
in terms of responses.

01:07:41.610 --> 01:07:44.160
We updated the model
so it's cheaper.

01:07:44.160 --> 01:07:47.370
We added kind of like tools,
specifically file search

01:07:47.370 --> 01:07:49.830
so it's grounded in our data.

01:07:49.830 --> 01:07:51.300
Now you've added evaluators

01:07:51.300 --> 01:07:54.210
so I can evaluate things
like friendliness.

01:07:54.210 --> 01:07:56.250
And I've run a batch evaluation

01:07:56.250 --> 01:07:58.560
and now I'm running
a red teaming scan.

01:07:58.560 --> 01:08:01.230
So this is kind of like,
and all of this I did

01:08:01.230 --> 01:08:03.570
in the Foundry portal without
ever leaving the browser

01:08:03.570 --> 01:08:05.580
or writing a single
line of code.

01:08:05.580 --> 01:08:08.190
This is the equivalent
of your test,

01:08:08.190 --> 01:08:10.110
go from test kitchen to table.

01:08:10.110 --> 01:08:13.470
Because with Foundry portal,
you've now been able to go

01:08:13.470 --> 01:08:19.070
down the end-to-end process
of building your agent,

01:08:19.070 --> 01:08:23.240
iterating on it to optimize for
various features, evaluating it.

01:08:23.240 --> 01:08:24.770
When the evaluations are
done, you'll be able

01:08:24.770 --> 01:08:27.020
to see what worked
and what didn't,

01:08:27.020 --> 01:08:29.840
and then using the results of
those evaluations to come back

01:08:29.840 --> 01:08:32.820
and iterate again to fix
the issues that you see.

01:08:32.820 --> 01:08:34.920
So we'll come back and check
out the evaluation results

01:08:34.920 --> 01:08:37.200
when they're done, you can
